Give this to claude: Perf bug: about 75% of each frame is spent waiting on glCheckFramebufferStatus in desktop Chrome/Brave

On desktop Chromium browsers (tested in Brave 1.98 / Chromium 156 on Linux, RTX 2000 Ada, NVIDIA 535, ANGLE on OpenGL), the game runs at 9–23 fps with frames that stall for up to 1.4 s, even with full GPU acceleration on. A Chrome DevTools trace shows where the time goes:

- fbo_bind_targets (wasm-function[45296], build 161-2) calls glCheckFramebufferStatus on every draw call, about 255 times per frame.
- In Chromium, WebGL runs in a separate GPU process, so each status check is a blocking round-trip: the browser stops and waits for the GPU process to finish all queued commands before it answers (CommandBufferHelper::Finish in the trace). Each check takes about 0.4 ms.
- Result: 4.1 s of the 5.4 s spent in requestAnimationFrame went to these checks. Script, layout and GPU rendering time were all small.
- Callers: Dev_DrawIndexedPrimitive (most), Dev_DrawPrimitive, Dev_DrawPrimitiveUP, Dev_Clear, Dev_Present, all going through draw → fbo_bind_targets.

The call pattern on every draw is the same: detach and re-attach the same color and depth-stencil attachments (framebufferTexture2D(..., null), then framebufferRenderbuffer(..., rb)) and check the status again. The final attachment state is identical every time.

Fix: in fbo_bind_targets, keep a cache of what's attached to each FBO: the render target and depth-stencil surfaces, and the texture or renderbuffer at each attachment point, with level and face.
1. If the requested targets match the cache, skip the glFramebuffer* calls and the status check entirely.
2. Only re-attach and call glCheckFramebufferStatus when an attachment actually changes. Better still, check only in debug builds or once when an FBO is first set up.
3. Clear the cached entry when a surface is destroyed or its storage is reallocated.

Proof: I patched it from the browser side by injecting a script that caches checkFramebufferStatus per FBO and only asks again when the effective attachments change. With that, the game holds 60 fps locked to vsync, the blocking checks per frame drop to zero, and rendering looks the same. The patch is below if useful:

// Cache WebGL checkFramebufferStatus while an FBO's effective attachments are unchanged
const FB=0x8D40, DRAW=0x8CA9, READ=0x8CA8, OK=0x8CD5;
for (const P of [WebGL2RenderingContext.prototype, WebGLRenderingContext.prototype]) {
  const o={}; for (const m of ['bindFramebuffer','framebufferTexture2D','framebufferRenderbuffer','checkFramebufferStatus','deleteTexture','deleteRenderbuffer','texImage2D','texStorage2D','renderbufferStorage','renderbufferStorageMultisample']) o[m]=P[m];
  const S=new WeakMap(), st=gl=>S.get(gl)||(S.set(gl,{draw:null,read:null,fbs:new WeakMap(),gen:0}),S.get(gl));
  const fbs=(s,fb)=>s.fbs.get(fb)||(s.fbs.set(fb,{att:new Map()}),s.fbs.get(fb));
  const bound=(s,t)=>t===READ?https://t.co/wzAoaZfi1R:s.draw;
  P.bindFramebuffer=function(t,fb){const s=st(this);fb=fb||null;if(t===FB)s.draw=https://t.co/wzAoaZfi1R=fb;else if(t===DRAW)s.draw=fb;else if(t===READ)https://t.co/wzAoaZfi1R=fb;return https://t.co/oPYOpl1Osh(this,t,fb);};
  const attach=(n,val)=>function(...a){const s=st(this),fb=bound(s,a[0]);if(fb){const f=fbs(s,fb);f.att.set(a[1],val(a));f.dirty=true;}return o[n].apply(this,a);};
  P.framebufferTexture2D=attach('framebufferTexture2D',a=>a[3]?['t',a[3],a[2],a[4]]:null);
  P.framebufferRenderbuffer=attach('framebufferRenderbuffer',a=>a[3]?['r',a[3]]:null);
  for (const m of ['deleteTexture','deleteRenderbuffer','texImage2D','texStorage2D','renderbufferStorage','renderbufferStorageMultisample']) P[m]=function(...a){st(this).gen++;return o[m].apply(this,a);};
  const sig=f=>{let k='';for(const[p,v]of f.att)if(v){f.ids??=new WeakMap();f.n??=0;const id=f.ids.get(v[1])||(f.ids.set(v[1],++f.n),f.n);k+=`${p}:${v[0]}${id}:${v[2]??''}:${v[3]??''};`;}return k;};
  P.checkFramebufferStatus=function(t){const s=st(this),fb=bound(s,t);if(!fb)return https://t.co/cYTha7tjvO(this,t);
    const f=fbs(s,fb);if(f.dirty){f.cur=sig(f);f.dirty=false;}
    if(f.okSig===f.cur&&f.okGen===s.gen)return OK;
    const v=https://t.co/cYTha7tjvO(this,t);if(v===OK){f.okSig=f.cur;f.okGen=s.gen;}else f.okSig=null;return v;};
}

The proper fix belongs in the C/C++ D3D-to-GL layer (fbo_bind_targets): skipping the redundant glFramebuffer* calls there also saves command-buffer work, which this JS patch doesn't. This should speed the game up for every desktop Chrome, Brave and Edge player.

